# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# ترجمة 01–11
T = {
"01": {
"en": """I am from al-Baradouni’s country. Ever since Alexander the Macedonian spread the rumor that my land is “Happy Yemen,” I have been searching for it in every inch of the earth. I am the one of whom al-Baradouni said: “A rock from the Dam, crossing the ocean to… a second, whose echo cries out: who has seen my pillars?!”""",
"notes": ["Abdullah al-Baradouni (1929–1999) is the celebrated blind poet of Yemen. “Happy Yemen” (al-Yaman al-Sa‘ida) is the old epithet for the country, the Arabia Felix of the Romans."],
},

"02": {
"en": """I am a Pashtun. I doubt you know them, and I hope you don’t. One rainy night my luck slipped off the mountains of Khost and fell into your hot country, where nothing is ever taken seriously. Everything in me has changed. I no longer carry a rifle, and I no longer leap across rocks like an ibex. Your country took my tent from me, and the udder of my ewe, and the bread of the tandoor. Nothing remains of me but my two green eyes, which stop people like you, the naive or the curious, so that I may confess: I am a Pashtun!""",
"notes": ["Khost is a mountainous province of eastern Afghanistan."],
},

"03": {
"en": """Hello, I am a Bengali. Please do not turn your lens away from my face, and put off summoning the stereotype of us for a few seconds, until I smile. Ever since the accursed Gustave Le Bon set down, with his ill-omened pen, his words on Bengal and its people, we have counted for nothing in the eyes of others. We are not cowards, nor hypocrites, nor scoundrels, nor unmanly, as that Frenchman with the moldy mustache claims. A man who has lived since birth among the tigers of the thickets, the crocodiles of the rivers, and the calamities that strike by night and by day is no coward. And he is no hypocrite who, if he wished to starve the world to death, could do it simply by leaving his hoe and his land to ruin. Our land bears fruit twice and three times a year; you eat from it and give no thanks, and then you despise us. I marvel at myself and at you: I work my land to feed you, and I work your land to feed my children. Forgive me, my employer is calling. Goodbye.""",
"notes": ["Gustave Le Bon (1841–1931), the French polymath, wrote disparagingly of the peoples of India in his writings on its civilizations; the speaker answers him directly."],
},

"04": {
"en": """Hello, I am from the land of al-Fitouri, whose last words in this world were: “Remember me, Africa.” Do you know him? Never mind. It is enough that you know how to count money; who cares about al-Fitouri these days? He died of a great grief whose name was “Africa,” yet he left the trembling of his hands everywhere. I am a son of the Nile. My boat moored on your shore in the days when boats still moored and camels still knelt. I have two daughters of marrying age. Imagine spending your life in the desert in toil that eats away the face and dyes the hair white, all for the sake of your own flesh and blood, only to have some green fool, who has never tasted the bread of exile or the tea of humiliation, pluck them from you. I am not old yet. I can still smile at jokes about politics, and thrill to “Halif al-Soun” and Mohammed Wardi. Why do I look younger in your lens? Perhaps my mirror needs rinsing with hot water, or perhaps I need an urgent vacation.""",
"notes": ["Muhammad al-Fituri (1936–2015), the Sudanese poet, wrote the poem “Remember Me, Africa.” Mohammed Wardi (1932–2012) was one of Sudan’s best-loved singers."],
},

"05": {
"en": """A Rashaida man, one of the Bedouin of Sudan. In his eyes there is a spring of temptation. Stare into them for a few moments and you fall captive to the wilderness and catch the contagion of wandering.""",
"notes": ["The Arabic says Rashidi, the singular form of the Rashaida, an Arab tribe of Sudan and Eritrea."],
},

"06": {
"en": """In the faces of laborers there is a language whose readers died in a terrible collision with prosperity. Only a few survived: those whom their mothers had charmed against harm with migration and the memory of their forefathers.""",
"notes": [],
},

"07": {
"en": """I am a butcher from West Africa. Herds of ill-fated livestock pass beneath the blade of my knife, to become, in the end, platters that whiten faces, with which you crown your happy nights. Everywhere on earth a butcher, besides taking the lives of animals, also sells the meat. Everyone but me. My job is limited to sending the animals’ souls to the next world and cutting the carcass limb from limb, so that afterward I may collect a few banknotes, and the entrails, if I am lucky. My livelihood runs in the veins of the sheep and hides from me in the throats of camels. I don’t know why my sustenance flees from me. Perhaps because I chase it with a knife!""",
"notes": ["To “whiten the face” is Arabic for bringing honor, here to the host whose table is well supplied."],
},

"08": {
"en": """I am Egyptian, beyond definition, short of excess. Everyone knows me and no one knows me. I am the hundred million in your eyes, and I am a speck of dust in my own land. The khamsin winds carried me over the sea, so that I wake again in exile and sleep every night on a pillow of hope. The night here is not long enough to think about a homeland the size of Egypt, “the gift of the Nile,” as Herodotus called it, nor are there stars enough for my mind to roam among. I am the hoarseness of al-Sitt in Azbakiyya and a sour breath from Sayyid Salim’s flute. I am a melody that al-Sunbati never notated, and a rhyme that escaped from a poem by Shawqi.""",
"notes": ["Al-Sitt (“the Lady”) is Umm Kulthum; Riyad al-Sunbati was one of her great composers; Sayyid Salim was a celebrated ney (reed flute) player; Ahmad Shawqi was the “Prince of Poets.” The khamsin is the hot spring wind of Egypt."],
},

"09": {
"en": """My name is Ramadan, and from my name I have had the fullest share of fortune. I was told that Ramadan in Arabic comes from al-ramd, which means the fierce heat. Ever since my father died, every month in my calendar has been a Ramadan. I could not believe it when the recruitment office in Dhaka called me, and I nearly went mad with joy. Of course, no one rejoices at leaving his homeland, unless his homeland had already left him. I fought off the pangs of longing that rushed at my heart as I closed my one suitcase, ready to travel. I consoled myself with a tomorrow promising a great deal of contentment: I would pay for my mother’s illness and feed my little brothers. That is how I killed my wish to run back toward my village as I climbed the steps of the plane to Jeddah. My sponsor is in his fifties, thick of mustache and of character alike, and owns a stall selling onions. I don’t know. My name is Ramadan, and I am afraid I will turn out to be the man who “fasted and broke his fast on an onion.”""",
"notes": ["The proverb “he fasted, and broke his fast on an onion” describes a long effort rewarded with almost nothing.", "The “sponsor” (kafil) is the employer who holds a foreign worker’s residency under the sponsorship system."],
},

"10": {
"en": """Stranger, you are the first person to point a camera at my face since the passport officer at the airport, when I first arrived in your country. A long life lies between the two shots, if you only knew. And you are the first person to call me beautiful since my wife. One of you must be lying. And then, I don’t know why you are so taken with the dry faces of my friends. Do we look to you like faces fit for photographing? I won’t hide from you that, as flustered as you make me, I feel warmly toward you, for few people give me a friendly chance to talk about myself, or press me to say more. But my dictionary is good for nothing except buying and selling and flattering my sponsor at the end of the month. Thank you, kind young man, and good luck in your life, of which I know nothing.""",
"notes": [],
},
}
